"I also tried:

eval <- eval_spatial(rasters$NDVI, where = fm_int(domain = list(
  geometry = smesh, t = 1:10), crop_area), layer = 1:10)
"

The layer information has to be given for _each_ element of "where". The integration points will have many spatial points for each time, so layer=1:10 cannot be correct.

eval <- eval_spatial(rasters$NDVI, where = fm_int(domain = list(

  geometry = smesh, t = 1:10), crop_area), selector = "t")

(which I see you tried afterwards)

Please provide the traceback() output after the

" Error in.subset2(x, i, exact = exact): subscript out of bounds"

error message so I can see where in the code it happens.
Also packageVersion("INLA"), packageVersion("inlabru") and packageVersion("fmesher")

Your tmesh is fine for use in the stmodel definition. It's just that the point process model you want has discrete time, and the covariate is not defined for non-integers even if the field component is.

Note:

"dic = TRUE,
                 waic = TRUE,
                 cpo = TRUE"

None of these are well defined for LGCP models (dic is _probably_ ok, but waic and cpo definitely are not, as they are based on leave-one-out cross validation, which is nonsensical for point process models.

